A||||4||fldevel||can a auto tranny overheat??||||1||
Z||000000||fldevel||11-23-2001||06:12 PM||||if so ... how can i tell ?? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||maryjane||11-23-2001||06:24 PM||||Absolutely yes. First indication is a smell from the fluid on the dipstick. If it isn't corrected, the color will be kinda brownish.<BR>Symptom may show up as a slight increase in the water temperature, because it puts more heat into the coolant in the radiator, or slipping during acceleration. The more it slips, the hotter the fluid gets, the more damage it causes. Continued overheating will cause transmission failure. Low fluid is one cause, as is a dirty transmission filter.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-23-2001||06:30 PM||||Yes it can over heat. But at the same time, it's hard to tell externally. The normal operating temperature should be between 150-200F.<P>The only way to get some idea of the temperature in the tranny would be to install a temperature sender and gauge. No, there isn't any easy way to do that. (Many modern PCM computers actually do monitor transmission temp.)<P>For spot checking you can use an IR pyrometer and measure oil pan temperature to get some rough idea.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fldevel||11-23-2001||06:41 PM||||ah ha . .thanks guys .. tranny isint slipping ..more like it isint shifting at the proper shift points .. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||grinthock||11-23-2001||07:08 PM||||When I changed my TC I had a tranny cooler put on to augment the exisiting stock system, I plan on putting on a temp gauge, easy to do, just put a TEE in the line and put the sender on the tee.||||reg||1
